{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Seattle Mariners arrived with genuine hope — a roster built around stars like Robinson Canó, Nelson Cruz, and the emerging Mitch Haniger, backed by what would prove to be one of the most dominant closers in baseball in Edwin Díaz. They delivered on that promise early, ripping through April and May with a combined 33-21 record, fueled by a barrage of timely home runs and clutch hits that announced Seattle as a legitimate contender in the American League West.\n\nJune was the Mariners at their very best. Going 19-9 in the month, they rode the bats of Haniger, Denard Span, and Jean Segura while their bullpen — usually — held the line. But cracks were appearing. Alex Colomé blew critical saves against the Yankees and Orioles, including a devastating three-run shot by Chris Davis that swung a game by nearly 0.6 win probability. Those moments hinted at a fragility beneath the surface, even as the wins kept piling up into late June.\n\nJuly broke the spell. The offense cooled, the bullpen frayed further, and the Mariners stumbled to a 10-13 record. August was worse — a 12-16 collapse that effectively ended their playoff hopes. The rotation couldn't stop the bleeding, and opponents feasted on a relief corps that had carried too heavy a load for too long. The dream of ending Seattle's 17-year playoff drought slipped away in the dog days of summer.\n\nSeptember brought bittersweet baseball — a 14-13 record that was competitive but ultimately hollow. Daniel Vogelbach's grand slam, Robinson Canó's clutch double off Mike Trout, and late heroics from Kyle Seager and Chris Herrmann gave the faithful something to cheer, but the Mariners finished 89-73, one of the best records in franchise history to miss the playoffs. It was a season that promised everything and delivered heartbreak — a familiar story in Seattle, rendered in a new and particularly cruel way.",
  "chapters": [
